Provide health/readiness checks.

# 5. PostgreSQL vs Elasticsearch responsibilities

PostgreSQL is the canonical source of truth.

Elasticsearch is a derived, rebuildable search index.

Never store data exclusively in Elasticsearch if losing the Elasticsearch volume would make the archive unrecoverable.

Article persistence flow should generally be:

1. persist/update canonical data in PostgreSQL
2. commit successfully
3. enqueue an Elasticsearch indexing job
4. worker updates Elasticsearch
5. PostgreSQL tracks indexing state/version

Elasticsearch must be completely rebuildable from PostgreSQL.

Use versioned Elasticsearch indices behind aliases, for example:

articles-v1
articles-v2

with an alias such as:

articles-current

This should allow future zero-downtime reindexing.

# 6. Core data model

Do not treat every RSS entry as an independent article.

Use a canonical Article entity.

A single article may be discovered through multiple feeds.

Implement an Article/Feed relationship table that can preserve feed-specific discovery information such as:

* feed_id
* original RSS GUID
* RSS title
* RSS description
* discovery timestamp
* feed-specific URL
* feed metadata

Separate article metadata from large extracted content.

Conceptually:

Article
ArticleContent
Feed
FeedFetch
FeedArticle
Entity
ArticleEntity
Keyword
ArticleKeyword
Country
StoryCluster
processing/indexing state

Do not blindly use these exact names if better naming is justified, but preserve the conceptual separation.

# 7. Article identity and canonicalization

Store both:

* original URL
* normalized/canonical URL

URL normalization should account for things such as:

* hostname casing
* fragments
* common tracking parameters such as utm_*
* known redirect/tracking wrappers where safe

Do not destroy the original URL.

Generate hashes for normalized titles and normalized extracted text.

Use those hashes to support duplicate detection.

Initial deduplication should prioritize:

* canonical URL
* normalized title
* exact content hashes

Near-duplicate/event clustering must run asynchronously rather than blocking initial ingestion.

# 8. Story clustering

Article and StoryCluster are different concepts.

If Reuters, BBC, AP, and another outlet all report the same event, those remain four independent Article records.

They may belong to one StoryCluster.

The clustering implementation must be replaceable behind an interface.

Initial clustering can combine:

* normalized title similarity
* extracted text similarity
* entity overlap
* timestamps

The UI should support concepts such as:

"Also reported by 7 other sources"

and allow opening the full story cluster.

# 9. Feed configuration

RSS source management is a first-class feature.

Users must be able to:

* add feeds
* edit feeds
* enable/disable feeds
* assign country
* assign expected language
* assign categories/tags
* configure polling interval
* configure article-fetching behavior
* inspect health/errors

Each feed should independently support modes roughly equivalent to:

* RSS only
* RSS + fetch full article
* RSS + fetch article + retain raw HTML

Feed polling interval must be configurable per feed.

Use conditional HTTP requests using:

* ETag
* Last-Modified

where supported.

Persist feed-fetch history including useful information such as:

* HTTP status
* fetch duration
* discovered entries
* new articles
* errors
* ETag
* Last-Modified
* timestamps
* next scheduled fetch

# 10. Fetching etiquette and security

Implement per-domain concurrency/rate limits.

Use a configurable, identifiable User-Agent.

Do not hammer news websites.

Protect article fetching against SSRF.

Validate URLs and schemes.

Prevent fetching clearly unsafe internal/private network destinations unless explicitly supported by configuration.

Apply conservative outbound-network defaults.

# 11. Ingestion pipeline

Treat processing as independently retryable stages.

Conceptually:

fetch feed
→ normalize feed entries
→ canonicalize
→ deduplicate
→ persist
→ optionally fetch article
→ extract content
→ NLP processing
→ clustering
→ Elasticsearch indexing

Do not build one huge transaction/job that must restart everything when one stage fails.

Jobs should be:

* idempotent
* safely retryable
* observable
* independently recoverable

Use exponential backoff.

After repeated failures, jobs should reach an explicit failed/dead-letter state rather than silently disappearing.

Provide administrative retry mechanisms.

# 12. Article extraction

Define an extractor interface.

Trafilatura is the default implementation.

Store extracted article text in PostgreSQL.

When raw HTML retention is enabled, do not store all raw HTML directly in PostgreSQL.

Use an object-storage abstraction.

Initial implementation:

* local filesystem / Docker volume

Architect the interface so an S3-compatible implementation can be added later without redesigning article storage.

Store the object reference/path in PostgreSQL.

# 13. Article version behavior

Do not implement full historical versioning of every article body in v1.

Maintain the current extracted content and enough metadata to indicate that the content changed.

Use content hashes and timestamps.

Do not silently overwrite without preserving useful change metadata.

# 14. Countries

Distinguish these concepts:

source_country
primary_story_country
mentioned_countries

Do not collapse them into one country column.

Use ISO country codes.

"Greek news source" and "article about Greece" must remain different concepts.

# 15. Languages

Architecture must be multilingual-capable.

Ship English processing first.

Feeds may declare an expected language.

Articles should also have a detected language.

Use standard language codes.

NLP processors should be able to declare which languages they support.

# 16. NLP architecture

Use a processor/plugin model.

Examples:

KeywordProcessor
EntityProcessor
CountryProcessor
KeyphraseProcessor

Avoid hard-coding all NLP logic into one worker.

Every generated NLP annotation must record enough information to determine:

* processor
* processor version
* relevant algorithm/model version

This is required so millions of articles can be selectively reprocessed later.

# 17. Keywords

Use normalized Keyword records and article-keyword relationships.

ArticleKeyword should be able to retain:

* frequency/count
* relevance/score
* algorithm
* processor version

Support user-editable global stop-word lists.

Do not rely only on simple token frequency.

Support layered processing such as:

* term frequency
* TF-IDF or equivalent
* keyphrases
* named entities

# 18. Entities

Use normalized Entity records.

Entity types should support concepts such as:

* PERSON
* ORG
* GPE/COUNTRY
* LOCATION
* EVENT
* PRODUCT
* OTHER

Do not use a rigid PostgreSQL enum that prevents new processor-defined entity types later.

ArticleEntity relationships should support useful metadata such as:

* occurrence count
* relevance
* processor/version
* offsets or references when practical

Optional local spaCy processing can be used for NER.

The application must still function if the spaCy processor is disabled.

# 19. Processing state

Do not use a single processed=true field.

Track major processing stages independently.

Examples:

* fetched
* extracted
* NLP processed
* clustered
* search indexed

Store failures and timestamps per stage where useful.

Provide mechanisms to re-run:

* extraction
* NLP
* clustering
* Elasticsearch indexing

for selected articles or date ranges.

# 20. Scale target

Design for approximately 5 million articles.

Do not overengineer for billions of records, but avoid architectural choices that obviously break at a few million.

Important considerations:

* appropriate PostgreSQL indexes
* bulk inserts/upserts
* efficient many-to-many tables
* avoid N+1 queries
* avoid OFFSET pagination for deep result sets
* avoid individual inserts for thousands of keywords/entities
* limit graph result sizes
* asynchronous large exports
* efficient Elasticsearch mappings

Do not prematurely partition every PostgreSQL table.

Keep the schema/migrations structured so high-volume time-based tables can be partitioned later if profiling shows it is necessary.

# 21. Identifiers

Expose stable UUID-style identifiers externally through the API.

For high-volume internal association tables, use efficient composite/integer keys when appropriate.

Do not require random UUIDs to be the physical primary/index strategy for every junction table.

# 22. Search

Search must be one of the strongest parts of the application.

Use Elasticsearch.

Default search should cover:

* title
* RSS description
* extracted body
* entities
* keywords

Support normal users who simply type:

NATO

but also support structured syntax such as:

"Nuclear weapons"
iran AND israel
source:reuters
country:GR
entity:"Donald Trump"
after:2026-01-01

Provide an advanced GUI filter builder so search syntax is not required.

Support filters including:

* time range
* source
* country
* language
* entity
* entity type
* keyword
* story cluster
* processing state where useful

Search results should support sorting by:

* relevance
* newest
* oldest
* most mentioned / appropriate popularity metric

Default ranking may combine Elasticsearch relevance with sensible recency weighting.

Return highlighted matching snippets.

# 23. Pagination

Use cursor-based pagination for large article/result collections.

Do not depend on deep OFFSET pagination such as:

OFFSET 4000000

Design API pagination for large archives from the beginning.

